---
import StoryLayout from '../layouts/StoryLayout.astro';
import StoryArt from '../components/StoryArt.astro';
import AgentMeta from '../components/AgentMeta.astro';
---
<StoryLayout title="For organizations · Froglet" description="A clearer way to collaborate across organizations: agree what to share, define access and limits, and build on reusable services. Explore what works today and what comes next." path="/managed/">
<AgentMeta slot="head" route="managed" />
<div class="story-wrap">
  <section class="story-hero story-hero--compact"><div class="story-hero__copy"><span class="story-kicker">For organizations · Our direction</span><h1>Start with an agreement.<br /><span class="story-accent">Build less infrastructure.</span></h1><p class="story-lede">Your team knows what it wants to share. Your partner knows what it needs. Making that collaboration work should not become a project of its own.</p><div class="story-actions"><a class="btn primary" href="#today">What can we do today?</a><a class="btn secondary" href="#direction">Where we are heading</a></div></div><StoryArt name="10-open-source" width={1536} height={1024} eager alt="Bob and Alice assemble a shared service from a few useful pieces." /></section>

  <section class="story-section story-split"><div><span class="story-kicker">The problem behind Froglet</span><h2>“We want to collaborate.”<br />Then the work stalls.</h2><p>Two teams use different names for the same thing. A useful table sits in a spreadsheet. A working analysis lives on one person’s machine. Everyone wants to share; someone still has to build and maintain the connection.</p><p>After 10 years building platforms in life sciences, this is the recurring problem that led Armanas to build Froglet.</p></div><div class="story-callout"><h3>A small, concrete starting point</h3><p>Bob’s lab has a table mapping its sample names to a shared vocabulary. Alice’s team needs that mapping for its own research.</p><p>Bob shares the selected lookup as a service. Alice can ask for a mapping from her own tools. The terminology still needs expert agreement; the service makes that agreed work reusable.</p></div></section>

  <section class="story-section" id="direction"><div class="story-heading"><span class="story-kicker">The direction</span><h2>Describe the collaboration.<br />Make it operational.</h2><p>The long-term goal is to turn an agreement into configured services that people and their assistants can use. These are the decisions that should drive the setup.</p></div><div class="story-grid">
    <article class="story-use"><span class="story-kicker">What</span><h3>The useful part</h3><p>Which data, fields, and operations are shared? What must stay out? What does the result mean?</p></article>
    <article class="story-use"><span class="story-kicker">Who & how</span><h3>The conditions of access</h3><p>Which partner can use it? For how long, for which purpose, and within what limits?</p></article>
    <article class="story-use"><span class="story-kicker">Commitments</span><h3>The terms of service</h3><p>Price, availability, support, permitted use, and what happens when something goes wrong.</p></article>
  </div><div class="story-callout" style="margin-top:36px"><p><strong>This is the product direction.</strong> Froglet does not currently read an arbitrary contract and implement all its clauses. SLA monitoring, remedies, and organization-wide policy management are future work.</p></div></section>

  <section class="story-section" id="today"><div class="story-heading"><span class="story-kicker">A clear starting point</span><h2>Useful controls today.<br />A bigger vision ahead.</h2></div><div class="story-grid story-grid--two">
    <article class="story-status"><span class="story-kicker">Available in the current beta</span><h3>Run a focused collaboration.</h3><ul><li>Prepare selected data or a small function.</li><li>Expose one existing tool operation with defined inputs and limits.</li><li>Set up invitations with expiry and request allowances.</li><li>Review publication, pause access, and inspect signed records.</li><li>Run the service on infrastructure you control.</li></ul><p class="story-note" style="margin-top:24px">Invitations and existing-tool connections require technical setup. Invitations cover one provider’s services; separate audiences need separate providers. Test the deployed access controls before relying on them.</p></article>
    <article class="story-status"><span class="story-kicker">Not available yet</span><h3>The managed organization workspace.</h3><ul><li>Translate a whole agreement into service configuration.</li><li>Manage partner permissions across an organization.</li><li>Monitor service-level agreements and apply remedies.</li><li>Run always-on managed hosting through a Froglet account.</li><li>Use a complete commercial billing workflow.</li></ul><p class="story-note" style="margin-top:24px">There is no managed product to sign in to today. You can evaluate the self-hosted building blocks now.</p></article>
  </div></section>

  <section class="story-section story-split"><StoryArt name="07-signed-record" alt="Linked records of an exchange, ready to inspect." /><div><span class="story-kicker">Keep an inspectable record</span><h2>Know what was agreed.<br />See what was returned.</h2><p>Froglet records signed commitments and results so an exchange can be checked later, including when the original session has ended.</p><p>A signed record establishes what a key signed. It does not guarantee that the work is correct, identify a legal signatory by itself, or resolve a dispute.</p><a href="/verify-receipt/">Inspect a sample record</a></div></section>
  <section class="story-closing"><span class="story-kicker">A practical first step</span><h2>Start with one useful exchange.</h2><p>Choose a small, non-confidential catalog or operation. Test it with a partner before widening the scope.</p><div class="story-actions"><a class="btn primary" href="/publish/">Prepare a first service</a><a class="btn secondary" href="/learn/share-services/">Read the implementation guide</a></div></section>
</div>
</StoryLayout>
